# Object/field label translation (inside the canonical deploy command)

English labels for custom objects, the standard Name field, and custom fields
are produced, validated, planned, deployed, and verified through the existing
orchestrator. Provenance is also stamped on standard field rows (OwnerId,
CreatedDate, …). There is no second translation command.

```
python scripts/prep_deploy.py --org "<ORG>" --tabs "<tab1>,<tab2>" --phase deploy \
    --apply-translations
```

`--apply-translations` is the gated sheet write of the translation batch
(preview first, then confirm). It is not a second pipeline.

<!-- Updated by Divakar N — 2026-10-26. Why: ERPPJ-1116 client questions
     included an English-label review section; the user confirmed English
     labels are not a client requirement. -->
## Who the English labels are for

English labels exist only for GDC developers who cannot read Japanese. They
are **not** a client requirement; the client's Japanese labels are the
deliverable.

- Never ask the client (Yagai-san or anyone on the client side) about English
  labels, and never put English-label items in client questions, Jira
  comments to the client, or blocker notes.
- Weak or unfitted English (DeepL `FIT` that lost meaning, `WARN` unfitted
  labels left out of the package) is reported to the user only, as an
  internal readability note. It never blocks a deploy.
- If a label needs fixing, a GDC developer may type English into
  `Field Label (EN)` (provenance `manual`), with the usual gated sheet write.
  The ban on shortening labels with an LLM still applies.

## Flow (inserted into prep_deploy, not a fork)

1. Live fetch of **selected `--tabs` only**.
2. Structural pre-validation + collect labels that need English.
3. Provider preflight: DeepL MCP if healthy, else Google Sheets `GOOGLETRANSLATE`.
   One provider per batch; never mix. DeepL failure after preflight is fatal
   (no Google fallback, no partial write). DeepL resolves its key without prompts
   from `DEEPL_API_KEY`, a locked-down `DEEPL_API_KEY_FILE`, macOS Keychain
   (`sf-deploy/deepl`), or `DEEPL_API_KEY_COMMAND`, in that order. The key remains
   in process memory and is passed only to the MCP child; Cursor `mcp.json` is not
   read. Tool arguments come from `tools/list` `inputSchema` (official
   `deepl-mcp-server` uses `sourceLangCode`/`targetLangCode` with `ja`/`en-US`).
   Stdio supports NDJSON and LSP Content-Length.
4. Terminology: API-name match → standard field map → unique JA→EN on the
   selected tabs → provider.
5. One confirmation for the complete sheet-write batch (headers, EN, provenance).
6. Stale-check immediately before write. Recalc + read calculated Google values.
7. Existing `validate_sheet.py --require-en` **plus** translation ERRORs
   (after enrichment, blank/invalid EN never degrades to WARN).
   <!-- Updated by Divakar N — 2026-10-07. Why: on a raw sheet, blank English
        is work for step 2, not a blocker. -->
   A standalone `validate_sheet.py` run (no `--require-en`) reports blank
   English as INFO `translation.pending`, not an ERROR. Invalid English
   (JA text, API name, placeholder) is still an ERROR in both modes.
   <!-- Updated by Divakar N — 2026-10-07. Why: a long English label is a
        wording problem DeepL can fix, not a reason to park the field. -->
   **English longer than 40 characters is NOT a blocker** (WARN
   `translation.length` in both modes). Step 3b handles it.
3b. **DeepL length fit** (`fit_overlength_labels` → `DeepLProvider.translate_fit`).
   Every Name / custom-field English over 40 characters, whether from the sheet,
   DeepL, or Google, is re-translated from the Japanese by **DeepL** with
   escalating `context` + `customInstructions` (`mcp_deepl.FIT_ATTEMPTS`).
   <!-- Updated by Divakar N — 2026-10-14. Why: DeepL output varies per call;
        one pass gave up on 減価償却費（含特償） (CompanyInfo row 23), which the
        first prompt fits in most calls, so the field shipped with no English. -->
   DeepL wording varies from call to call, so one pass is not enough: the
   attempts are repeated for `mcp_deepl.FIT_ROUNDS` (3) rounds, on the
   Japanese as written and on its half-width (NFKC) form, and the first result
   of 40 characters or fewer wins. A label is reported unfitted only after
   every round fails. DeepL is used here even in a Google batch. This
   is the only exception to "one provider per batch". The fitted label is
   written to the sheet with `deepl` provenance inside the same confirmed batch
   (preview line `FIT`). If DeepL cannot fit it or is unavailable, the sheet
   cell stays as is, the plan holds that label out of the package
   (`MISSING_TRANSLATION`), and the preview shows a `WARN`. The field itself
   still deploys. A Google result over 40 is not an abort either; the next run
   fits it. Never shorten a label by hand or with an LLM.
8. Fresh org snapshot (Tooling fields + CustomObjectTranslation).
9. Immutable plan → schema XML + patched COT XML (org tree preserved).
10. Manifest **from the plan**. Check-only, then real deploy, then live exact-EN verify.
11. Sync state (org Id) only after verification succeeds.

## Sheet columns (header-driven)

Object header (same object tab, typically row 1 — above the field list):
- `表示ラベル` (col A) / Japanese value (col D) = CustomObject singular+plural master
- Object English = that **same header row** in the `Field Label (EN)` column
- No provenance is generated for the object name (header row). Existing header
  EN / Name EN cells are never overwritten for this.
- If header object English is **longer than 40 characters**, the org package
  uses the standard **Name** field English (`nameFieldLabel` row) for
  `CustomObjectTranslation.caseValues` singular. The sheet is left as-is.
- Plural `caseValues` (`plural=true`) is an English plural of that singular
  (slash compounds pluralize each side, then fit ≤40). Tokens that are already
  plural (e.g. `details`) are left unchanged. It is not copied from the
  singular and is not written to the sheet.
- Object **Description** (`説明`) stays Japanese on the sheet. English for the
  org is written only to `CustomObject.description` (not Translation Workbench,
  not the sheet). DeepL can fill that in-memory EN; **Google batches skip it**
  because GOOGLETRANSLATE would require a sheet cell. A sheet `説明` that is a
  layout note (record types / validation rules / lookup filters removed, add
  later) is NOT the object description and must not be deployed. Description
  translation does not force an otherwise translation-only existing object into
  the schema package; it lands when that CustomObject is already in scope.

Field list (from the `fullName`/`type` header down to `END[項目]`):
- `Field Label (EN)`
- `Translation Provenance` — one cell immediately to the right of
  `Field Label (EN)`: `origin | ja-sha256 | generated-at`
  (`manual` / `inventory` / `deepl` / `google`). Stamped on custom **and**
  standard field rows. Legacy three-column headers are still read if present,
  never created.

Missing headers occupy spare `FreeColumnGDC*` cells after confirmation.
Japanese source changes (hash mismatch) replace previous English, including
former `manual` values. Unchanged JA is never retranslated because DeepL later
became available.

## Out of initial scope

Picklist values, relationship labels, help text, LWC/Flow/Custom Labels, layouts,
record types.

## Must not

- Write English into Japanese `CustomField.label`.
- Ask the client to review, supply, or fix English labels.
- Deploy blank translations.
- Use API name / JA source / placeholder / formula text as English.
- Scan or modify tabs that were not selected.
- Change `fetch_sheet.py` / `write_back.py` Google authentication outside
  `scripts/google_auth.py` (the shared login helper, see `google-sheet-auth.mdc`).
- Call Cloud Translation API, a service-account JSON, or an LLM to generate
  English labels.
- Use Cursor/Claude/another LLM to generate translations.
